Selling Software as Shareware

The shareware system is among the least expensive channels to distribute 
computer software.  By encouraging computer users to download shareware 
from on-line services and distribute copies to their friends, the 
shareware author can easily reach an enormous audience without incurring 
large software distribution costs.

Getting the users to pay the shareware fees, however, has proven to be 
more difficult.  To persuade users to register the shareware, authors are 
willing to accept very low fees; usually in the $10 to $20 range.  Most 
active users of a shareware program feel its price is very reasonable.

Still, many user don't pay the shareware fee simply because they don't 
want to take the time to:
1. Write a check,
2. Fill out a registration form,
3. Address an envelope, and
4. Mail it.

If this process was simplified, many more users would register the 
shareware.

One way to simplify the process is to use an electronic payment system 
that allows users to pay the shareware fees by e-mail.  The system, 
called the NetBank, uses electronic payment coupons that may be exchanged 
via e-mail.

If you have not already read the detailed description of the NetBank, 
it may be requested by sending e-mail to: netbank-info@agents.com, 
as described at the end of this document.

Summary of the NetBank System:

The NetBank system is an on-line transaction system that provides a 
global exchange mechanism for electronic payment coupons.  The payment 
coupons, called NetCash, are initially bought from a nationwide 
distribution service, then traded electronically using e-mail.  NetCash 
coupons may be spent by anyone, and anyone with access to e-mail may 
accept them as payment.

Users buy NetCash coupons by calling a nationwide "pay-per-call" 
900-number.  The use of a 900-number was chosen because it is fast and 
convenient, and, unlike credit cards, it is available to all telephone/
modem users.  This level of convenience encourages people to use 
NetCash to pay for shareware and other information services.

Once a user has a NetCash coupon, they may spend it by sending it via 
e-mail.  Anyone may accept NetCash, and anyone may "cash it in". The 
charges incurred to distribute the NetCash are deducted when the NetCash 
is converted back to real money.

The simplicity of the system, and the ability to pay for shareware 
electronically, appeals to many computer users.  Both the shareware user 
and the shareware author benefit from this system.

