Encryption

NetBank users may access the NetBank from any e-mail address on the
Internet. Since most e-mail users are not using encryption, the NetBank is
designed to respond to un-encrypted (plaintext) messages.  While this
technique is convenient, it suffers from the same problem as sending cash
via the U.S. Postal System.  If, during transit, someone comes in contact
with the cash, they have an opportunity to "steal" it.

Since the ownership of NetCash is based on the knowledge of the unique
serial number embedded in it, anyone who sees this number may spend the
NetCash.  To eliminate this problem, it is strongly recommended that all
NetBank e-mail messages be encrypted.


"Privacy Enhanced Mail" and "Pretty Good Privacy"

Support for several encryption technologies will be available at the
NetBank in the near future.  This will include support for a "public key"
encryption standard.  Users with a preference of PEM-compliant verses PGP
style should state their preference.  Please send comments to:
encryption@agents.com.

We are currently developing tools to allow the NetBank to use PEM and PGP. 
We will announce support for PEM and/or PGP as soon as it is available.  We
feel it is vital for the NetBank to support PEM and/or PGP.  And, once
people start using public key encryption for all of their e-mail
correspondence, no additional "processing" of NetBank messages will be
necessary.


Simple Encryption

In the mean time, a simple encryption technique may be used to "disguise"
the NetCash embedded in your mail messages.  This technique should be
sufficient to deter casual e-mail snooping.

The technique uses a simple (easy to implement) "secret key" algorithm. 
Each user generates their own secret key and delivers it to the NetBank
(via U.S. Mail or PGP encrypted e-mail).

The encryption algorithm is based on the "Exclusive-Or" boolean operation. 
By using the exclusive-or operation, the same processing is used to both
encrypt and decrypt a message.  Encrypting a message that is already
encrypted will decrypt the message (if the same key is used both times). 
Also, this algorithm is very easy to implement, and provides good
protection when encrypting "random" sequences of digits (as in a NetCash
serial number).  

The key consists of any sequence of ASCII characters. To provide an
adequate level of security, the key should be at least as long as a NetCash
"coupon" (30 or more characters).  Encryption keys consisting of random
characters are better that words or names.  But, it's your choice. 

Once the NetBank has your "secret key", you may encrypt your e-mail
messages before you send them to the NetBank.  The NetBank will use the
same key to encrypt the reply message before sending it to you. 

You should perform the following steps on your mail messages before they
are sent to the NetBank.

1. Encrypt the message using the exclusive-or algorithm (listed below).
2. Encode the encrypted message (using UUENCODE) so it may be sent via e-mail.

Example message (plaintext):

   NetCash $ 10.00 B1234H5678W /Accept

Example message (encrypted and uuencoded):

   begin 660 msg
   D'2`W$20G.V5G<G1D?75S<@=E879W&G!B9'T4<FH5,"8F(C%>
   `
   end

In this example the encryption key is the string "SECRET".  The 'begin' and
'end' lines are used during the uudecoding process.

Utility programs that UUENCODE and UUDECODE are available for most computer
systems.

The source code for the exclusive-or encryption/decryption program
(cipher.c) is listed below.  An example UNIX command line to encrypt,
uuencode, and mail a transaction to the NetBank is:

  echo "NetCash $ 10.00 B1234H5678W /Accept" | cipher SECRETKEY |
     uuencode msg | mail netbank@agents.com

where:
  SECRETKEY is the secret key you have sent to NetBank.


When you receive the reply from the NetBank, you will perform the following:

1. Extract the reply message from your mail system, if necessary, to allow 
   further processing.
2. Decode the reply message (using UUDECODE) so it may be decrypted.
3. Decrypt the reply message using the exclusive-or algorithm.


-------------------- cipher.c --------------------

/*
 * cipher.c -- a simple data encryption/decryption utility
 *
 * This program uses the exclusive-or '^' boolean operator to perform
 * encryption and decryption.
 * The encryption key is specified on the command line.
 * Input is read from stdin, and output is written to stdout.
 */

#include <stdio.h>

main(argc, argv)
        int argc;
        char *argv[];
{
        int ch, len;
        long i = 0;

        if ( argc != 2 ) {
                printf ( "Usage: %s <key>\n", argv[0] );
                exit(1);
        };

        len = strlen( argv[1] );

        while ( (ch = getchar() ) != EOF ) {
                putchar( ch ^ argv[1][i++ % len] );
        }
}

