Answers

1. What is the NetBank, and what will it do for me?

The NetBank is an electronic payment system that allows you to buy and sell
"information products" via electronic mail.  The system uses electronic
payment coupons (NetCash) that are exchanged for goods and services using
e-mail.  For example, you can easily pay a shareware software author by
sending him NetCash in an e-mail message.  And the shareware author can
immediately send you the latest version of his software.  There are, of
course, many other uses for NetCash.

If this interests you, please read the "netbank-intro" and
"netbank-commands" documents described above.  They will answer many
questions that are not in this FAQ.

If you are NOT interested in an electronic payment system, you need not
read any further.


2. How you I get NetCash?

The first step in using the NetBank system is to obtain some NetCash. 
There are two ways to get NetCash.  The first way applies if you are
selling a product or service on-line; you simply accept NetCash from your
customers as payment.  The NetCash you accept from your customers may be
cashed-in or spent on other products or services.  The second way to get
NetCash is to buy it.
  
You may buy NetCash using the on-line distribution service, or by prepaying
with a check or money order.

On-line Distribution Service  

NetCash may be bought by calling a "pay-per-call" 900-number.  You must use
your computer and modem to make the call.  Your modem should be set to
8-N-1, and at a speed between 1200 and 9600 bps.  Once the connections is
established, you will be prompted to type a "C" to complete the call.  This
is how you confirm your understanding that the cost of the NetCash will be
charged to your telephone bill.  You should do so rather quickly; if you
don't type a "C" within 5 seconds, you will be disconnected (without
charge, of course).

If you type a "C", you will be issued a $10 NetCash coupon, and your phone
bill will be charged $10.

Your NetCash will look something like this:

        NetCash $ 10.00 E1234H5678W

Each call to the NetBank issues a single NetCash coupon.  If you need more
than $10.00 you will need to make multiple calls to the 900-number.  Below
is the 900-number and the standard usage restrictions:

        NetCash coupons by telephone:
        900-933-CASH  $10.00 per call.
        Personal computer and modem required.
        Must be 18 years or older or have parental permission.
        Service provided by Software Agents, Inc.

Prepaid Discount Service

Instead of charging NetCash to your telephone bill, you may prepay for
NetCash by sending a check in the mail.  You will receive a 10% discount
off the cost of the NetCash.  To buy NetCash by mail, send a check or money
order to:

        Software Agents, Inc.
        NetCash Distribution Center
        P.O.Box 541
        Germantown, MD 20875

Any amount of NetCash may be purchased by mail.  Once your order is
processed, the NetCash coupons will be sent to you via electronic mail.  Be
sure to include your e-mail address with your order.

Since NetCash is immediately spendable, the distribution center must wait
for personal checks to clear before distributing the coupons.  To avoid the
delay, it is recommended to use a certified check or money order.


3. Will my NetCash coupons be accepted by everyone?

No, not everyone accepts NetCash.  However, a growing number of on-line
information services and shareware software publishers are accepting
NetCash.  Feel free to ask on-line "merchants" if they accept NetCash for
payment.  It is definitely easier e-mailing them a NetCash coupon, than
sending them a check in the mail!


4. How do I make a purchase using NetCash?

Purchases are made by exchanging NetCash coupons for information products
or services.  This process usually occurs via electronic mail, or some
other type of on-line communications.  

Okay, let's assume that you called the 900-number and have a NetCash
coupon.  What do you do with it?  One thing you can do is spend your
NetCash at any "merchant" that accepts NetCash.  You simply send your
NetCash to the merchant in exchange for their services.  Before sending
your NetCash, you must make sure that you have "exact-change".  That may
involve requesting change from the NetBank.

For example, suppose you want to buy something for $5 and all you have is
the $10 coupon you got from the 900-number. The first thing you do is
"change" your 'ten' for two 'fives'.  This is done by sending a mail
message (NetCash transaction) to the NetBank.  The message should be
addressed to: netbank@agents.com, and the following line should appear in
the message:

        NetCash $ 10.00 E1234H5678W /change 2 fives 

The NetBank will send a "receipt" to you via e-mail.  This receipt will
contain two $5 coupons. 
 
        NetCash $ 5.00 E2431H7878W
        NetCash $ 5.00 E4321J8765W

Note:  When the NetBank receives your $10 coupon, it is "consumed" during
the process of making change.  The original $10 coupon is now void, and may
not be spent again.

Okay. You now have a $5 coupon that you may use to pay whoever it is that's
selling whatever it is your buying.  When you give your coupon to this
person, they will accept it by sending an e-mail message to the NetBank. 
Once they accept your coupon they have your $5, and your coupon becomes
void.


5. What is the maximum dollar amount that may be traded with NetCash?

The NetBank system was designed mainly to support small, low cost exchanges
of money.  The NetBank allows you to use e-mail to buy on-line information,
or pay shareware fees for software.  These types of transactions are
usually in the $10 to $20 dollar range.  Also, NetCash can be used to send
25 or 50 cents to merchants who are selling electronic newsletters via
e-mail.

However, to answer the question, there currently is no maximum limit on the
size of a NetCash transaction.  But, if you are buying something that's
expensive, you probably don't want to pay with NetCash (or real cash, for
that matter).


6. What NetCash denominations are available, and how do I make "change"?

NetCash is available in the following standard denominations: quarters
($0.25), ones ($1), fives ($5), tens ($10), and twenties ($20). Two other
denomination are available, but are rarely used.  They are fifties ($50)
and hundreds ($100).

The "change" command is used to make change.  The command is appended to a
NetCash coupon to form a transaction.  This is explained, in detail, in the
"netbank-commands" document.  The command consists of the keyword "change"
followed by one or more change requests.  A change request consists of the
requested number of coupons and the requested denomination.  The following
transaction contains two change requests:

        NetCash $ 10.00 E1234H5678W /change 1 five 5 ones

You may also exchange multiple "small" denomination coupons for "larger"
ones.  This is done by using the "accept" command.  When you accept
multiple NetCash coupons in a single mail message, the NetBank summarizes
the transactions and issues the minimum number of new coupons necessary. 
For example, sending the following two transactions to the NetBank:

        NetCash $ 5.00 E2431H7878W /accept
        NetCash $ 5.00 E4321J8765W /accept

will return a single coupon:

        NetCash $ 10.00 E4321H5678W


7. How do I cash-in my NetCash coupons?

Cashing in a NetCash coupon is easy, but, like cashing in foreign currency
at a real bank, there is a charge for the conversion.  That said, anyone
may cash-in NetCash coupons.  To do so, they must open a NetBank Merchant
account.  Once they have their account, they request reimbursement of their
NetCash by sending a "deposit" transaction to the NetBank. For example, the
following transaction will deposit a $5 coupon into account number 12345:

        NetCash $ 5.00 E4321J8765W /deposit 12345

Once a month, the NetBank totals the reimbursement requests for each
account, and a check is sent to each merchant with a non-zero account
balance.  The cost of distributing NetCash via the 900-number is deducted
before each check is written. The 900 service provider deducts their fee
(about 20 percent) before they distribute funds to the NetBank, so the
NetBank only collects a about 80 percent of the NetCash "face value".  The
NetBank must pass these charges to the merchant who cashes-in the NetCash. 
There is a definite advantage to merchants spending their NetCash on other
merchants, or selling it to their customers, instead of cashing it in at
the NetBank.  Remember, NetCash is "traded" at full face value and with no
transaction fees.

And yes, currently an account with only 25 cents in it would get a check,
if that's the total balance at the end of the month.  Since postage is 29
cents, and there are bank charges to process checks, this will have to
change in the future.  To issue a check, a minimum balance of $5 to $10
should probably be in the account.  But for now, any non-zero balance gets
a check.


8. What is a NetBank Merchant?

A NetBank Merchant is any person that wishes to cash-in NetCash.  Usually a
merchant will sell an information product or service and collect NetCash
from their customers.  If you are running a business using the NetBank, you
will want to cash-in some of the NetCash you receive.


9. Do I pay a service charge every time I spend or accept NetCash?

No, there is no service charge to process NetCash transactions.  The
NetBank processes transactions that allow users to accept NetCash, make
change, and request reimbursement (deposit), all without a charge.  Since
NetCash may be traded in very small amounts (25 cents), it is vital that
there are no transaction fees.

 
10. What about someone guessing a NetCash serial number and spending MY cash?

We expect a few people will try to steal other people's NetCash by guessing
their serial numbers.  To minimize this, we closely monitor all "rejected"
NetCash transactions to detect random guessing activity. 

Also, we can increase the length of the NetCash serial number to reduce the
chance of someone guessing it.

For added security, NetBank users may encrypt their NetCash transactions
before mailing them to the NetBank.  This is described in the
"netbank-encryption" document.


